# rules: eval-use
# languages: php
# fixture: php/CWE-95.php
# from: php/lang/security/eval-use.yaml, CWE-95, CWE-94
#
# eval() compiles its argument as PHP and runs it here: same scope, same
# includes, same database handle, same session. There is no sandbox and no
# lesser version of the outcome - a string that reaches eval is the program,
# so the only eval worth keeping is one whose argument is written in the file.
#
# The rule was `eval($$$_);` minus `eval('$$$_');`, and neither half worked. A
# PHP pattern whose arguments contain no named hole is not read as PHP at all -
# the grammar takes it for inline HTML text and the query looks for those
# characters verbatim - so the first half matched nothing. The second is worse
# than nothing: quotes and arity are both lost from it, leaving "any call with
# any arguments", so had the first half worked the second would have subtracted
# all of it. See GUIDE.md; this is the failure that ran through most of the PHP
# corpus.
#
# What works is a hole in the function position and a guard on what it caught,
# which is also the shape to write when a rule is about a list of names.
#
# "Written in the file" has to be read the way PHP reads it. A single-quoted
# string interpolates nothing, so `eval('$e = new Exception(...); throw $e;')`
# is a constant however many dollars it contains - PEAR does exactly that, and
# a guard that treated any $ as interpolation reported it. A double-quoted
# string is the opposite: a $ or a { in one is code.

["$F($CODE)", "$F($CODE, $$$_)"] as $calls
| "^(?:'(?:[^'\\\\]|\\\\.)*'|\"[^\"$\\\\{]*\")$" as $written_here
| scan_ast("*.php"; $calls)
| where_capture("F"; "^(?:eval|create_function)$")
| where_capture_not("CODE"; $written_here)
| finding("eval-use";
    "this compiles $CODE as PHP and runs it in the current scope; anything "
    + "that reaches it is the program from that line on")
| report
